        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The human skeleton undergoes a continuous renewing process. Under normal conditions a balance exists between breakdown and reformation of the bone material. There is evidence that space flight upsets the system and causes an imbalance, even during short-duration missions. Furthermore, the cumulative effect of multiple short-term flights may be similar to that of extended exposure to microgravity. This is a major concern for the health of astronauts.</p>
<p>Scientists believe that reduced mechanical work loads on the body in space induce the skeleton to discard bone it no longer needs. It is believed that the process of bone resorption begins almost immediately upon reaching microgravity. Researchers feel that the temporal relationships in calcium/bone homeostatic and endocrine responses will serve to prove or disprove this hypothesis. The purpose of this investigation is to define the response of the human calcium/bone homeostatic system to microgravity and gather data that will allow prediction of consequences for the health of astronauts.</p>
<p>Previous investigations flown on the Spacelab Life Sciences 1 and 2 (SLS-1 and SLS-2) missions (see experiment titled Pathophysiology of Mineral Loss During Space Flight) verified that the human calcium regulatory hormone system functions normally in space. The increased calcium levels in the bloodstream, from increased bone breakdown, cause higher excretion of calcium in urine and a decrease in calcium absorption in the intestines. A higher calcium intake - the therapy for osteoporosis on Earth - is expected to cause further urinary calcium excretion and increases the risk of kidney stones for space travelers.<p>
APPROACH:<br>
From 10 days before launch until 7 days after landing, each participating astronaut ingested a nonradioactive, stable isotope of calcium (48Ca) with each meal. The labeling was done to distinguish between calcium coming from the diet and calcium being resorbed from bone material.
Samples of blood, urine and feces were collected. The samples collected during the space flight were frozen at -20° Celsius. The ratios of calcium isotopes (48Ca/40Ca) were measured after landing. In addition, the astronauts kept a log book, tracking their food and drug intake as well as exercise sessions.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Bone resorption increases relatively early in space flight (3-5 days). Serum ionized calcium is increased as a result and remains elevated throughout the duration of the flight. The normal adaptive responses of a decrease in serum PTH as ionized calcium is increased occur. The effect of a short term exposure to space flight is still manifest two months postflight with increased bone turnover and possibly elevated serum calcium.<p>
        </div>

